Subject: [PATCH 10591/11171] File the substrate-string gap found validating
 milestone 2026.09.598's artifacts

exp6836's live re-check warned SUBSTRATE_HAS_NO_DURATION_FLOOR on the free-text
substrate "deterministic CPU compilation". Following that led to a sharper case:
experiment_3399_logicvault_long_context declares inference_substrate "gpu",
duration_s 0.0035, and a terminal "complete:" verdict with no acceptance gate.
The bare string matches no recogniser, so no floor applies and DURATION_TOO_SHORT
cannot fire on a 3.5-millisecond GPU claim. The canonical live_llm_inference would
have imposed 60s.

Measured corpus-wide: 3,255 artifacts declare a substrate, 2,317 match a floor, 938
match none across 463 distinct strings. The entry states plainly that 938 is an
UPPER BOUND and not a defect count -- hardware_smoke's 201 are exempt by design,
and a blocked_* artifact declaring live_llm_inference at 0.0s is correctly clean
because nothing ran.

My first filter for "compute-sounding and under 60s" returned 73, and most were its
own negation-blindness: no_live_inference, no_new_inference, and
cached_aggregation_only_no_live all contain "live" or "inference" while denying
compute. Recorded as unvalidated rather than quoted as a finding. That is the bug
class the QA-Layer discipline names, committed in the tool doing the measuring.

Not actioned, deliberately. Adding "gpu" to the recogniser list is the wrong reflex
-- CLAUDE.md forbids growing that list to make things pass, and no fixed list covers
463 free-text strings. The real options are to escalate the existing warn when a
terminal verdict meets a short duration, or to require a canonical value and fail
closed. Both change what is quarantined corpus-wide and need their own measurement.

